WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:19.320 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  [Music]  hey everyone  i'm john coogan and this is my analysis  of the cyberpunk 2077 launch fiasco  to be clear this isn't a game review  channel by any means  so i'm going to be focusing on the  business of cyberpunk i've been enjoying  the game for a few weeks now  but i completely understand why people  who bought this game with high  expectations are frustrated  regardless of what you think about the  game there are some important lessons  about launching a product that every  entrepreneur should pay attention to  so that's what i'm going to focus on  today let's start with a little history  cyberpunk is an action rpg created by a  polish company called cd project  the company is the largest video game  maker in poland  but is actually fairly small by american  standards  they are currently worth 8 billion  dollars which is about the same as  discord  they aren't really a startup though  since they've been around since the mid  90s  but because their business involves  launching entirely new games on a  regular basis  each new title they release acts  somewhat like a small startup  cd project has an interesting backstory  the founders started by selling  cracked copies of western video games in  warsaw poland as kids  once they got older and started wanting  to do business legitimately  they focused on importing games from the  united states  as demand for american games grew in  poland they started offering translation  services to american game developers  so that the games could be enjoyed by  polish people who didn't speak english  this business grew with the overall  rising popularity of video games  and it paved the way for them to start  making their own games cd projekt's main  hit has been the witcher franchise  which includes three main games as well  as a few expansions and spin-offs  it's also been adapted into a netflix  original series starring henry cavill  cd project was in the perfect position  to work on the witcher because the  author of the books that the series is  based on  is polish and cd project is a polish  company  the witcher series has been a massive  success earning multiple game of the  year awards  and helping cd projekt go public on the  polish stock exchange  after seeing so much success with the  witcher adaptation  cd projects set out to find new  intellectual property to base their next  game off of  they settled on an old tabletop  role-playing game from the 1980s called  cyberpunk  expectations were immediately sky high  for cyberpunk 2077  and cd projekt did nothing to calm fans  down they brought keanu reeves on to  play one of the main characters  and even had him hype the game to fans  at e3 just look at how he sold the game  cyberpunk  is set in a metropolis of the future  where body modification has become an  obsession  you play as an outlaw an enhanced  mercenary  working in the sleazy underbelly of the  city  okay but let me tell you the feeling  of of being there of walking the streets  of the future  is really going to be breathtaking but  when the game finally launched after  multiple delays  it was riddled with bugs and was nearly  unplayable on current generation  consoles like the ps4 and xbox one  textures were low resolution animations  were buggy  and some problems would even kill your  character instantly  or even crash the game entirely  generally the game was a mess  and fans weren't happy cd projekt's  stock dropped  20 percent after the launch and they  quickly began offering refunds to gamers  who were unhappy with their purchase  many online stores began issuing  warnings to customers about the game's  issues  and sony even went as far as to pull the  game entirely from their online store  my experience with the game has been  generally positive i'm a big fan of the  cyberpunk aesthetic  and i like action rpgs as a genre while  i have experienced a few bugs while  playing  i'm running the game on a custom-built  pc with a high-end graphics card  so most of the issues haven't been a  problem i'm also not the type to binge  play a game the day it comes out  so i've been able to avoid the worst  bugs just by downloading updates  i'm also more forgiving of bugs  generally since i'm an early adopter by  nature  and this isn't the first game by cd  projekt to have issues at launch  when they launched the first witcher  game it was far from perfect  they actually released an enhanced  edition of that game a full year later  which upgraded the graphics and fixed  many bugs  and they pulled off something even more  impressive with the third installment of  the witcher  when the witcher 3 was released in 2015  for pc  ps4 and xbox one it was widely called  one of the best video games ever made  and was praised for its expansive open  world four years later  cd projekt was able to port the game to  the nintendo switch  a portable gaming console with a  fraction of the computing power of the  other platforms  so if anyone can get cyberpunk to  eventually run smoothly on older  hardware  it's cd project but while i don't doubt  that the engineers and game designers at  cd projekt can build a great game  i do have reservations about their  marketing strategy by endlessly hyping  the game  they really damage their reputation in  the gaming community over 8 million  people had pre-ordered the game and they  expected a flawless experience on day  one  if a game company is uncertain about  their ability to deliver a polished  product at launch  they usually release into early access  allowing gamers to play a limited  version of the game  this lets them get tons of real world  feedback and test for bugs across  a much wider range of hardware but  launching into early access  can be humbling for a big company and it  can also be less profitable  often early access games are sold at a  discounted price  and it can be hard to deploy a huge  marketing budget effectively  when the game has already been kind of  available for fans  as we've seen time and time again  there's very little substitute for real  world feedback  video games are the perfect medium for  rapid iteration and that's why most of  the highest grossing games of all time  are services that add features  consistently over time  games like minecraft roblox and fortnite  have all grown in public with real world  players testing new features  as they roll out but these games have  the advantage of not being primarily  narrative driven  the cyberpunk fiasco is so interesting  to me because it has several  deeply ironic aspects to it first  the game's narrative focuses on themes  of inequality and corporate abuse  but the game only runs well on expensive  custom-built pcs  leaving gamers who can't afford the  latest gaming hardware behind  to be fair this isn't entirely cd  project's fault  they began the development of cyberpunk  eight years ago  that's before the release of the  original ps4  development took so long though that by  the time the game was ready  the ps5 was already available and these  two consoles have significantly  different capabilities  the graphics processing unit in the ps5  is almost six times as powerful as the  ps4s  and it has twice as much memory so  making a game that takes advantage of  next-gen hardware like the ps5  will naturally lead to some compromises  on legacy consoles  but i wonder how deeply the team at cd  projekt thought about all this  it seems odd to write a whole story  about the problems of inequality  while releasing a product that will  ultimately leave less wealthy customers  unsatisfied i think they're doing the  right thing by offering refunds to these  customers  but it feels like they should have  delayed the console release entirely  it's not uncommon for games to launch on  one platform and then expand to other  platforms later  like cd projekt did with the nintendo  version of the witcher  doing this pushes revenues further out  into the future though and that has a  real business cost  the second ironic twist here involves a  particular bug some gamers have been  experiencing  in certain situations if the player gets  too many items in their inventory  the file that saves their progress will  be corrupted entirely  forcing them to start the game over from  the beginning  this is ironic because without spoiling  the story  one of the major plot points in the game  involves a kind of ticking time bomb  it's funny that there's a race against  the clock both in the main story of the  game  as well as the real world you have to  finish the game before gathering too  many items  or you might lose your progress and have  to start over  i think the story of the cyberpunk  launch should be instructive to  entrepreneurs who are launching anything  new  it always pays to get your product into  the hands of real users as soon as  possible  we saw quivi make the terrible mistake  of launching blindly without feedback  and it cost them the entire company even  with a few successful launches under  your belt  you aren't exempt from real world  feedback so try to beta test new  products with your most dedicated users  before you announce a specific launch  date early signs point to cyberpunk  still being a profitable game  even with the launch debacle but it's  unclear how much this will hurt cd  projekt's reputation in the future  hopefully they continue improving the  game until it becomes a fan favorite  but only time will tell so have you  played cyberpunk  let me know what you think about the  game in the comments below and if you  enjoyed the video  please consider subscribing to the  channel i don't plan on covering video  games regularly  but when i see an interesting business  case about a game i'll talk about it  so thanks for watching

